turning now to a story about  perseverance five siblings now calling  las vegas home after leaving their  parents behind in afghanistan in order  to escape the taliban victoria saha has  this exclusive story you will only see  right here on 8 news now  imagine being stripped away from the  only home and culture you know and be  placed in refugee camps until you  finally get to call a place home while  for these five siblings calling las  vegas home was quite the journey from  the moment they left kabul over the  summer  the world watched in horror when the  people of afghanistan tried to make a  desperate flee from the taliban regime  it was like completely die like  everybody don't care about dying but the  fakiri siblings were amongst the crowd  being shoved and pushed to make it  closer to the kabul airport gates out of  respect for their culture we will not be  showing the teen girl's face  we was waiting like for eight eight days  outside of airport  just in front of the door of airport in  capital fifteen-year-old fahim fakiri  tells me through a translator he is the  oldest amongst the five siblings the  youngest is five the american army only  let them through  we didn't saw like my father and mother  like  in that time we just everybody pushed  like the  running the heartbreaking journey alone  to america started for the siblings once  in qatar a call came to their uncle who  lived in las vegas  the case manager reached out to me and  said that your  your niece and nephews are here are you  taking the responsibility for us to  travel them to the united states the  answer was an instant yes  i could not live with myself  and be able to have that piece of bread  and piece of food for myself to eat that  knowing the fact that there's five  innocent kids without a destiny without  a future somewhere four months of  paperwork and legalities finally allowed  the siblings to make their way to las  vegas  when i saw them at the airport it was an  overjoy of you know overwhelming joy as  the siblings adjust to life in their new  home they spend their time with their  cousins and play cards  and sing  but they say they worry about their  parents  we miss them  so it's so difficult for me  so we can't forget them  the home the family now lives in came  thanks to a local charity called the ari  foundation the siblings are able to  communicate with their parents regularly  but say things back at home are still  very unsafe denise
 

